# Command line

The Ada executable is invoked as `eml`. The front-end is chosen from the **effective input format**: `--input-format` / `-if` when set (always wins), otherwise the **input extension**. Supported formats: `mxeml`, `teml`, `eml`, `beml`.

## Global options

- `--no-color` — plain-text diagnostics and usage on stderr (no ANSI color). Allowed before or after the subcommand.
- `--no-logo` — suppress the startup banner. Allowed before or after the subcommand.

## Shared input / output options

On `preproc`, `tokenize`, `parse`, and `compile`:

- `--input` / `-i <file>` — optional; if omitted, read **stdin** (text via newline-normalized path; **beml** via raw bytes / `Stream_IO`, never `Text_IO`)
- `--input-format` / `-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml` — required when `-i` is omitted; optional when `-i` is present (then overrides the extension). Space-separated only (no `--input-format=`); repeated `-if` is invalid CLI
- `--output` / `-o <file>` — optional; if omitted, command output goes to stdout
- `--output-format` / `-of` — optional; values depend on the command (see below). Space-separated only (no `--output-format=`); repeated `-of` is invalid CLI. When `-o` is present, the extension **must match** the selected `-of`
- `--format` / `-f` — **removed**; always invalid CLI (`unexpected --format/-f (use --output-format/-of)`)

Unknown `-of` for the current command is invalid CLI. `-of` on `help` is invalid CLI. Unknown extension without `-if` → error listing the extensions valid for that command.

Compile header `-- Source:` uses the `-i` path, or `<stdin>` when reading stdin.

## Shared preprocessor options

On `preproc`, `tokenize`, `parse`, and `compile`:

- `--var` / `-v $VARNAME=EXPRESSION` — repeatable; `$` required; split on first `=`; empty EXPRESSION is invalid CLI; repeated name is invalid CLI. Meaningful only for **mxeml** / **teml** (preprocessor on). On **eml** / **beml**, every `--var` is unused
- `--warn` / `-w default|none|error` — default is `default` when omitted
  - `default` — print unused `--var` warnings (yellow stderr); do not stop
  - `none` — suppress unused `--var` warnings
  - `error` — treat unused `--var` as error (red stderr); stop before next stage

## Input formats

| `-if` / extension | Role | Preprocessor |
|---|---|---|
| `mxeml` / `.mxeml` | Mathematical expression language (operators, functions, constants, `eml(x, y)`) | on |
| `teml` / `.teml` | Nested tree text only: `1` and `eml(S, S)` | on |
| `eml` / `.eml` | Textual stack IR (`ONE` / `EML`, `--` comments) | off |
| `beml` / `.beml` | Packed-bit binary stack IR | off |

## Command × format

| Command | Allowed inputs |
|---|---|
| `preproc` | `mxeml`, `teml` |
| `tokenize` | `mxeml`, `teml`, `eml` (not `beml`) |
| `parse` | all four |
| `compile` | all four |
| `run` | all four |

Incorrect payload (bad tokens, bad magic, malformed stack, extra bytes, …) → diagnostic + exit `1`, **no output file**. Lex errors on `tokenize` still dump valid tokens then fail. `parse` / `compile` / `run` stop before writing (or printing a result) on lex/parse/format errors.

## Diagnostics

Every warning and error is emitted via `Eml.Diagnostics` as:

```
[00001] line:column description
```

Optionally append ` (from $VARNAME)` when the location comes from substituted text. CLI / I/O diagnostics with no source location use `0:0`. BEML diagnostics use line `1` and a 1-based byte offset as column. Color: red for errors, yellow for warnings; `--no-color` disables. CLI errors still print usage + `Try 'eml help'…` after the diagnostic line(s).

## `help`

```
eml help
eml help preproc
eml help tokenize
eml help parse
eml help compile
eml help run
```

- `eml help` — usage, command list, input formats, and common options on stdout; exit `0`
- `eml help <command>` — command-specific usage on stdout; exit `0`
- Unknown topic → stderr diagnostic + usage, exit `1`
- Banner is still printed unless `--no-logo` is set

## Startup banner

Unless `--no-logo` is set, `eml` prints a one-line banner to **stdout** (never stderr, never the `--output` file):

```
EML compiler and interpreter - v0.1.<short-hash> - by Stefano Anelli
```

`<short-hash>` is the embedded git commit; `<major>.<minor>` comes from the crate version (e.g. `0.1.0-dev` → `0.1`).

With `--output`/`-o`, the banner still goes to stdout while command output goes only to the file. Without `-o`, stdout is the banner (unless `--no-logo`) then the dump; use `--no-logo` for a pure dump (pipes, tests).

## `preproc`

Substitute `$VARNAME` from `--var` bindings and write expanded **mxeml** or **teml** text.

```
eml preproc [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--output-format|-of mxeml|teml] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo]
```

- Effective input must be `mxeml` or `teml`
- `--output-format` / `-of` — optional; `mxeml` or `teml`; **default = effective input format**. Must match the input format (cannot preproc mxeml to teml or the reverse)
- `--output` / `-o` — optional; if present must end in `.mxeml` or `.teml` matching `-of`; if omitted, output goes to stdout
- Unbound `$VARNAME` — error for **every** occurrence; no output; exit `1`
- Unused `--var` — warning (or error with `--warn error`)
- No `$VAR` in file — output equals input (after newline normalization)

Examples:

```
eml preproc -i f.mxeml -v '$X=1+2'
eml preproc -i f.teml -o out.teml
eml --no-logo preproc -if mxeml < in.mxeml
eml --no-logo preproc -i f.mxeml -v '$X=1' -w none
```

## `tokenize`

Optionally preprocess (mxeml/teml), then dump the token stream. Input is optional (stdin); output is optional.

```
eml tokenize [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file.tokens>] [--output-format|-of tokens] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo]
```

- Effective input must be `mxeml`, `teml`, or `eml` (not `beml`)
- `--output-format` / `-of` — optional; only `tokens` (default `tokens`)
- `--output` / `-o` — optional; if present must end in `.tokens`; if omitted, the dump goes to stdout
- Flag order after `tokenize` is free; space-separated only (no `--input=`)
- On `eml` input, preprocessor is off; unused `--var` warns (or errors with `--warn error`)

### `.tokens` dump format

One token per line, no header, no EOF token, no skipped whitespace:

```
<line>:<column> <KIND> <lexeme>
```

`line` and `column` are 1-based **original** source positions. Around each run
of tokens from the same substitution occurrence (mxeml/teml), the dump inserts comment lines
(not tokens):

```
-- $VAR1 begin
1:8 NUMBER 1
-- $VAR1 end
```

Invalid tokens are not written to the dump.

### Errors and exit codes

- Invalid CLI → diagnostic `[ID] 0:0 …` + usage on stderr (red unless `--no-color`), plus a hint to try `eml help`, exit `1`
- Missing command → diagnostic for missing/unknown command, exit `1`
- Preprocess unbound `$VAR` → diagnostic per occurrence; no dump; exit `1`
- Unused `--var` → unused-variable warning or error; exit `1` only in `--warn error` mode
- Lex errors → diagnostic per bad token, optionally `(from $VAR)`; continue scanning; still emit valid tokens; exit `1`
- I/O failure → stderr diagnostic, exit `1`
- Success → exit `0`

Usage text (errors):

```
Usage:
  eml <command> [options]
  eml help [command]
  eml preproc [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo] [--output-format|-of mxeml|teml]
  eml tokenize [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo] [--output-format|-of tokens]
  eml parse [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo] [--output-format|-of mermaid|md|dot|svg]
  eml compile [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo] [--output-format|-of eml|beml|js|c|clib]
  eml run [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo]
Try 'eml help' for more information.
```

Examples:

```
eml tokenize -i filename.mxeml
eml tokenize -i f.eml -o other.tokens
eml tokenize -if teml < in.teml
eml --no-logo tokenize -if eml < prog.eml
```

## `parse`

Build a tree and dump it. **mxeml** dumps the expression AST; **teml** / **eml** / **beml** dump the IR EML tree.

```
eml parse [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--output-format|-of mermaid|md|dot|svg] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo]
```

- Effective input may be any of the four formats
- `--output` / `-o` — optional; if omitted, the dump goes to stdout
- `--output-format` / `-of` — optional; default `mermaid`. Values: `mermaid`, `md`, `dot`, `svg`
- Flag order after `parse` is free; space-separated only (no `--input=` / `--output-format=`)
- Parse `-of` / `--output-format` as their own flags so `-of` is never treated as `-o`
- On mxeml/teml: preprocess first. On eml/beml: no preprocess; unused `--var` warns/errors per `--warn`

### Output format vs extension

When `-o` is present, the extension **must match** the selected format:

| `-of` | `-o` extension | Contents |
|---|---|---|
| `mermaid` (default) | `.syntaxtree` | Raw Mermaid `flowchart TD` |
| `md` | `.md` | Markdown with a fenced Mermaid block |
| `dot` | `.dot` | Graphviz `digraph` |
| `svg` | `.svg` | Self-drawn SVG (no Graphviz) |

Mismatch is invalid CLI. Without `-o`, the same format is written to stdout.

Pipeline (by format):

- **mxeml** — read → preprocess → tokenize → parse AST → dump AST
- **teml** — read → preprocess → teml tokenize → teml parse → IR dump
- **eml** — read → eml tokenize → eml parse → IR dump
- **beml** — read bytes → beml reader → unflatten → IR dump

Stop with no tree on unbound/`--warn error`, lex, or parse errors. Parse errors stop at the first error (diagnostic via `Eml.Diagnostics`).

Exit `0` on success; `1` on invalid CLI, I/O, preprocess, lex, or parse errors.

`eml help parse` — parse-specific usage on stdout; exit `0`.

Examples:

```
eml parse -i filename.mxeml
eml parse -i f.teml -of md -o other.md
eml parse -if beml -i prog.beml
eml parse -i filename.mxeml -o other.syntaxtree
eml parse --no-logo -i filename.mxeml -of svg -o other.svg
```

## `compile`

Lower / reconstruct to IR EML and write textual `.eml`, binary `.beml`, browser JavaScript `.js`, or C (`.c` / library `.c`+`.h`).

```
eml compile [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--output|-o <file>] [--output-format|-of eml|beml|js|c|clib] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo]
```

- Effective input may be any of the four formats
- `-o` / `--output` — optional; extension must match `--output-format` (default `beml` → `.beml`); omit → stdout
- `--output-format` / `-of` — optional; default `beml`; values `eml`, `beml`, `js`, `c`, or `clib`
- `--var` / `--warn` / `--no-color` / `--no-logo` — same shared rules
- Flag order free; space-separated only (no `--output-format=`)
- Repeated `--output-format` is invalid CLI
- `eml help compile` → usage on stdout, exit `0`
- Unknown `-of` values (including future `cli`, `bytecode`, `binary`, `wasm`, `wat`, and the spelling `javascript`) are invalid CLI
- Exit `0` on success; `1` on invalid CLI, I/O, preprocess, lex, parse, or same-format errors

### Same-format compile (rejected)

- effective input `eml` and `-of eml` → error, stop, no output
- effective input `beml` and `-of beml` → error, stop, no output (including default `-of beml` on a `.beml` input)
- `mxeml` / `teml` → any compile output is allowed
- `eml` → `beml` / `js` / `c` / `clib` and `beml` → `eml` / `js` / `c` / `clib` are conversions

Output extension must match format:

| `-of` | `-o` extension |
|---|---|
| `beml` (default) | `.beml` |
| `eml` | `.eml` |
| `js` | `.js` |
| `c` | `.c` |
| `clib` | `.c` |

### JavaScript (`-of js`)

- Emits a classic browser script: header `//` comments, `function eml(x, y)` using math.js (`math.subtract(math.exp(x), math.log(y))`), and `function main()` returning nested `eml(...)` / `math.complex(1, 0)` from the IR tree (no Flatten, no top-level `main()` call)
- When `-o` is set, also writes a companion `.html` beside the `.js` (same basename) that loads a pinned math.js CDN bundle, then the generated `.js` (simple filename in `<script src>`), and displays `math.format(main())` in the body
- Without `-o`, only the JavaScript goes to stdout (no HTML)

### C program (`-of c`)

- Emits a standalone C source: `/*` header comments, `#include <complex.h>` / `<stdio.h>`, `static long double complex eml(...)` using `cexpl` / `clogl`, and `int main(void)` that evaluates the nested IR expression and prints with `printf("%Lf%+Lfi\n", creall(z), cimagl(z))`
- No companion files; `-o` optional (stdout if omitted)

### C library (`-of clib`)

- Emits a library `.c` defining non-static `eml` and `compute` (nested IR return); **no** `main`
- When `-o` is set, also writes a companion `.h` (same basename) with include guard, `#include <complex.h>`, and prototypes for `eml` / `compute`; the `.c` `#include`s that header by simple name
- Without `-o`, only the `.c` goes to stdout (include name `eml_generated.h`; no header file written)

Pipeline (by format):

- **mxeml** — preprocess → tokenize → parse AST → lower to IR → encode
- **teml** — preprocess → teml tokenize/parse → IR → encode
- **eml** — eml tokenize/parse → IR → encode (as beml / js / c / clib only, given same-format rule)
- **beml** — beml reader / unflatten → IR → encode (as eml / js / c / clib only, given same-format rule)

Stop with no output file on unbound/`--warn error`, lex, parse, or same-format errors.

Examples:

```
eml compile -i filename.mxeml -o other.beml
eml compile -i f.eml -of beml -o out.beml
eml compile -i filename.mxeml -o other.eml -of eml
eml --no-logo compile -if mxeml < in.mxeml -of eml
eml compile -i f.mxeml -v '$X=1' -w none -o out.beml
eml compile -i f.mxeml -of js -o out.js
eml compile -i f.mxeml -of c -o out.c
eml compile -i f.mxeml -of clib -o out.c
```

Native / .NET / JVM / wasm / wat backends (`-of cli`, `bytecode`, `binary`, `wasm`, `wat`) are not implemented yet.

## `run`

Interpret IR EML on a Long_Float complex stack and print one compact complex value on **stdout**. No file output.

```
eml run [--input|-i <file>] [--input-format|-if mxeml|teml|eml|beml] [--var|-v $NAME=EXPR]... [--warn|-w default|none|error] [--no-color] [--no-logo]
```

- Effective input may be any of the four formats
- `--output` / `-o` and `--output-format` / `-of` are invalid CLI (dedicated diagnostics, then usage)
- `--var` / `-v` and `--warn` / `-w` — same shared rules as compile. Meaningful only for **mxeml / teml** (preprocessor paste). On **eml / beml**, every `--var` is unused
- `$NAME:VALUE` extras are **not implemented** (still `CLI_Unexpected_Argument`)
- `eml help run` → usage on stdout, exit `0`
- Exit `0` on success; `1` on invalid CLI, I/O, preprocess, lex, parse, or runtime errors

Pipeline (by format): same front-end as compile (`Load_IR`), then `IR_Eml.Flatten` → evaluate `ONE` / `EML` → print `Format_Complex`.

Examples:

```
eml run -i filename.mxeml
eml run -i f.teml -v '$X=1'
eml --no-logo run -if eml < prog.eml
eml run -i f.beml
```

Do not invent extra subcommands or flags beyond this surface unless the user asks.
